{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>These are the words, etc.</b> ״אֵלֶּה״ excludes what preceded — meaning, since it says ״אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר מֹשֶׁה״ that they are his own words, for the entire book is rebukes and admonition from Moses to those who transgress the word of HaShem, and they said, of blessed memory (Megillah 31b): the curses in Mishneh Torah Moses said from his own initiative. And even what he went back and explained of the preceding utterances of HaShem — he was not commanded to do so, but on his own he went back over the matters. And Scripture was concerned lest one say that just as Moses said such things from his own initiative, so too in the preceding utterances Moses said some thing from his own initiative. Therefore it says <b>אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים</b> — meaning, these alone are the words that Moses spoke of his own initiative, but everything that preceded in the four books he did not say even a single letter on his own; rather, the words that came from the mouth of the Commander in their exact form, without any change even of a single extra or missing letter." "<b>Further,</b> Scripture further intends to tell us that all forty years that Moses shepherded Israel in the wilderness, he did not speak harshly to them other than these words — these being what he spoke harshly. And although we find that he said to them (Numbers 20:10) ״שִׁמְעוּ נָא הַמּוֹרִים״, he did not say this to the entirety of Israel but to one faction who were instructing a ruling, and so they say in the book of the Zohar. But to all Israel he did not speak harshly other than these. And this is what it is precise in saying <b>אֶל כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל</b> — meaning, to all of them, that he did not speak harshly except these; but to some of them one may find that he spoke harshly besides these." "<b>Further,</b> it further intends to allude to a reckoning of days on which he rebuked Israel, which are the numerical value of ״אֵלֶּה״. And so the verses prove that on the first of the month of Shevat he began to speak to them, as it is written ״בְּעַשְׁתֵּי עָשָׂר חֹדֶשׁ בְּאֶחָד לַחֹדֶשׁ״, and he passed away on the seventh of Adar (Kiddushin 38a) — that makes thirty-six, corresponding to the numerical value of ״אֵלֶּה״, during which he spoke the words of the rebukes stated in this matter." "<b>Further,</b> it further alludes, in the manner of what they said in the Gemara (Yoma 19b): Rava said: ״וְדִבַּרְתָּ בָּם״ — and not in idle words; end of quotation. From here [we learn] that it is forbidden to speak anything other than words of Torah and awe [of Heaven]. And Scripture informs us that Moses our teacher, peace be upon him — these alone are the words that he spoke all his days on his own initiative, besides the words that HaShem commanded him to speak. From this you learn that he fulfilled ״וְדִבַּרְתָּ בָּם״, and these words of his — every observer will testify that they are all words of Torah, wisdom, and admonition." "<b>Further,</b> it further alludes in this manner: ״אֵלֶּה״ — it disqualifies all words besides these, for no rank is equal to these words. And Scripture gives the reason for their superiority: (1) ״אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר מֹשֶׁה״ — on account of the excellence of the speaker, than whom there is none higher, as HaShem testified regarding the two of them, his brothers, as written in the Torah (Numbers 12:8). (2) ״אֶל כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — on account of the excellence of those addressed: a complete nation. And it further alludes in its saying <b>כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל</b> — meaning, not to Israel who were present there in that generation alone were these words, but to all Israel who were there and who were destined to be. For Moses established in these supernal words of his, upon all Israel, the obligation to fulfill every matter written in these words. See then that there are no words in the world of a rank equal to these words, and that is the import of <b>אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים</b> — meaning, and none compare to them." "<b>Which Moses spoke.</b> Meaning: For we find that he reproved Israel concerning what they rebelled against at the Sea of Reeds, and the objector will say: But those who rebelled at the Sea of Reeds had already died and were completed on the fifteenth of Av of the fortieth year (Eikhah Rabbah, Proem) before this statement of Moses — if so, what did this flock do that he should reprove them? Therefore it says <b>which Moses spoke</b> — meaning, if the words had been from the mouth of HaShem there would be no place to speak harshly to them, but Moses is the one speaking, and he can also discipline even those who were then under twenty years of age upon whom the decree had not been pronounced, for a court below punishes from thirteen years of age; and it was concerning those who were thirteen at the Sea of Reeds that Moses spoke harshly." "<b>To all Israel.</b> The reason it says <b>to all Israel</b> is that in Moses' words there are two matters: first, that he restored the Torah to them; second, that he reproved them for what they had already done. And for each matter the words needed to be addressed to all of them — regarding the matter of restoring the Torah, that is, the reason it was necessary to restore the things to them was because he was concerned about some person in Israel who had forgotten some commandment among the commandments of HaShem, and there was no way to grasp the identity of the one in error or what the error was; therefore he gathered them all and restored the Torah to them, so that through this means each person would have his deficiency in hand. Also regarding the matter of reproof — if he reproved only some of them, they could protest: how do these differ from those? Further, it is possible that the statement <b>to all Israel</b> included also those who had already died, for in his reproofs he spoke concerning the earlier ones and concerning the later ones, in the manner of what He said (Isaiah 43:27): ״אָבִיךָ הָרִאשׁוֹן חָטָא״." "<b>Across the Jordan.</b> He delayed reproving them until they were across the Jordan, when they saw that they had arrived at the region of their desire — for then they would accept his discipline — and not beforehand, when they were far from hope and their spirit was short and they would not incline their ear to understand his reproofs." "And the verse's saying <b>in the wilderness, in the Arabah</b> etc. — our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Sifrei) that he reproved them for what they did in the wilderness etc. And it is possible to interpret the entire verse by way of allusion in another manner, namely, that in this brief verse Moses taught the totality of the fear of HaShem and the fitting traits required for those who walk in the Torah of HaShem, and they are nine traits." "<b>The first)</b> is that one should adopt the trait of Abraham, as it is written (Genesis 14:13): ״לְאַבְרָם הָעִבְרִי״ — and this is what he alluded to by saying <b>across (be'eiver)</b>." "<b>2)</b> That there should be submissiveness in his heart always, as they said of blessed memory (Berakhot 7a): one act of submissiveness in a person's heart is better than a hundred lashes. And this is what he alluded to by saying <b>haYarden.</b>" "<b>3)</b> The trait of humility, as they said of blessed memory (Eruvin 54a): a person should always regard himself like a desert. And see what I explained in parashat Yitro on the verse ״וַיִּסְעוּ מֵרְפִידִים״ (Exodus 19:2). And this is what he alluded to by saying <b>baMidbar.</b>" "<b>4)</b> That the humility should be by way of pleasantness, not by way of repulsive degradation, as the Rambam wrote in Hilkhot De'ot (chapter 5). A further condition of humility is that one should not use it to avoid giving reproof for sin, if he sees a person transgressing the word of HaShem and says, ״Who am I and who is my house to reprove great men״; rather, one must reprove by way of the obligation of mutual responsibility (arvut). And this is what he alluded to by saying <b>ba'Aravah</b> — comprising two things: 1) that the humility should be pleasant (areivah), as it says (Proverbs 3:4): ״וּמְצָא חֵן וְשֵׂכֶל טוֹב״ etc.; 2) the obligation of mutual responsibility (arvut), that one must reprove for sin, whether small or great, and not use humility for this matter." "<b>5)</b> The saying of the Tanna (Avot 3:1): Reflect upon three things, etc. — and before whom you are destined to give account, etc. And likewise the Tanna said (Avot ch. 2): Repent one day, etc., and he explained there that the intent is that one should always remember the day of his death, and that every day is a time of the boundary of death. And this is what he alluded to by saying <b>mul Suf</b> — that the end and final outcome of a person should be before his eyes." "<b>6)</b> The saying of the pious (Hovot haLevavot, Sha'ar haPerishut – Sha'ar 9), and this is its language: his cheerfulness on his face and his anxiety in his heart. And this is what he alluded to by saying <b>bein Paran uvein Tofel</b>: corresponding to cheerfulness on his face he said <b>bein Paran</b> — denoting pe'er (splendor), the opposite of sadness, which is a gloomy countenance; and corresponding to anxiety of the heart he said <b>uvein Tofel</b>, that his heart should not be at ease with him, but rather he should be anxious in his heart out of fear lest he transgressed the word of HaShem unwittingly, whether in great matters or in small ones, or lest he fell short in divine service from what he is obliged to do." "<b>7)</b> That he have a pure and clean heart, in the manner that David prayed (Psalms 51:12) ״לֵב טָהוֹר בְּרָא לִי״ and so forth, and that he distance from himself hatred of creatures and jealousy and competition and malice, and this is what he alluded to by saying <b>ve-Lavan</b> — that he should have a pure heart, so that he attribute to purity this designation of לָבָן." "<b>8)</b> Study of Torah with constancy, like Jacob our father, as it is written (Genesis 25:27) ״יוֹשֵׁב אֹהָלִים״ — not to study casually, and this is his saying <b>va-Ḥatzerot</b> — they are the courts of HaShem, which are the houses of study." "<b>9)</b> That he not run eagerly after things that resemble the love of the wealth of this world, for all who follow the desire of his heart is idle from the service of HaShem, and a person must suffice himself with what is necessary. And this is what he alluded to by saying <b>ve-Di Zahav</b> — that he say to the gold: ״enough.״ Or it may intend along this path, that whatever he has should be in his eyes a sufficient thing, as though he has all the gold, and this is along the lines of the saying (Avot 4:1): ״Who is rich? One who rejoices in his portion,״ and thereby his heart will incline toward the supernal service, which is the service of HaShem, the living Elohim."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:01.260462397Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}